and researchers in britain have made a  very welcome discovery in the fight  against covid a low-cost  widely used anti-inflammatory has  improved the chances of survival for  seriously ill patients now this is the  first treatment so far  to show life-saving promise the medicine  dexamethasone  has reduced deaths by a full third among  patients on ventilators the drug though  does not bring any relief to patients  with milder  symptoms hospital staff are struggling  to help severely ill covered 19 patients  in the uk  though these images were recorded  several weeks ago scenes like these  are still being played out around the  world every day  brings more deaths but now  a glimmer of hope the worst hit country  in europe is hailing a major  breakthrough  scientists at oxford university say they  have identified the first drug that can  save the lives of covid19 patients  dexamethasone is a widely used steroid a  randomized trial showed it reduced death  rates by a third in patients on  ventilators  and by a fifth in patients receiving  oxygen  it really is showing quite a significant  effect and it's really important because  the drug itself  is very widely available it's on almost  every pharmacy shelf in every hospital  it's available throughout the world and  it's extremely cheap  so we've looked at the numbers and if we  treat eight patients in intensive care  with this drug  we'll save one life and the total cost  of treating all eight patients is only  about 40 pounds  40 pounds is about 50 us dollars the  drug works by reducing inflammation in  the lungs  the study results are still to be peer  reviewed but the uk  lost no time in approving it for use in  the country's health service  we've been stockpiling it since march so  we now have two hundred thousand courses  that are ready to go and we're  working with the nhs so that the nhs  standard treatment  for 19 will include dexamethasone  from this afternoon  as a generic drug dexamethasone is  readily available around the world  it's not a cure for covid19 but with no  vaccine yet available and infection  rates rising in many parts of the world  its potential is welcome news  so what exactly do we know about  dexamethasone and how the steroid  combats covert 19 well we put that  question to jason kindred chuck  he's a professor in virology at canada's  university of manitoba  so one of the things that we tend to  look at with these different types of  emerging viruses uh is the fact that  they tend to  basically increase the immune response  so essentially get an over stimulation  of  of the immune response which in most  cases actually is protective for us  but in many cases can actually increase  disease severity  so the hope with something like  dexamethasone is that rather than acting  directly on the virus and trying to  reduce the ability of the virus to  create more copies of itself  what the drugs are trying to do is  essentially reduce all that damage that  uh is part and parcel of an old  overactive immune system  we're trying to learn about this in real  time and we know that some of the  initial data that had come out  uh at the very start of this pandemic  had suggested that steroids  uh actually may exacerbate uh disease in  in those that are in the early stages  so i think one of the concerns right now  with with methazone  is that uh there you know would there be  the potential that if you treated  patients too early  that you might actually end up  increasing uh the the potential severity  of disease because it actually acts as  an anti-inflammatory so  i i think that we we have to look at  this as a potential game changer  for those patients that are actually in  severe disease distress  but we are still uh lacking for some  sort of a therapeutic  that will ultimately help reduce  infections in the very early stages of  disease
 

